.TH EXCUBITOR 1 "August 2026" "excubitor @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
excubitor \- cooperative GPU lock manager for a shared multi-GPU node
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B excubitor
.I command
.RI [ options "] ..."
.br
.B excubitor
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.br
.B excubitor
.RB { \-v | \-\-version }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B excubitor
is a cooperative, flock\-based lock manager for GPUs on a shared
multi\-user node. Locks are per\-GPU\-index, backed by
.BR flock (1)
on files under
.IR LOCK_DIR " (" /var/lock/excubitor ")."
.PP
This is COOPERATIVE only: it stops nothing at the kernel/driver level on
its own. It only works if everyone uses
.B excubitor
instead of launching CUDA jobs directly. The node should also have
NVIDIA's
.B EXCLUSIVE_PROCESS
compute mode enforced as a hard backstop, and
.BR domestikos (1)'s
periodic check running to catch and log anyone who bypasses
.B excubitor
anyway \(em see
.BR domestikos (1)
for the admin side of this.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "run " "{\-n <num_gpus>|\-g <gpu_id>[,<gpu_id>...]} [\-t <giveup_sec>] \-\- " command " ..."
Acquire GPU(s) and run
.IR command ,
releasing them when it exits.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-n " num_gpus"
Request any
.I num_gpus
free GPUs. Defaults to 1 if neither
.B \-n
nor
.B \-g
is given.
.TP
.BI \-g " gpu_id[,gpu_id...]"
Request specific GPU(s) by index instead of a count, e.g.
.BR "\-g 0,2" .
All of them must be free together \(em partial availability doesn't
grant any of them, and the wait/give\-up behaviour below applies to the
whole set. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-n .
.TP
.BI \-t " giveup_sec"
Give up waiting after
.I giveup_sec
seconds if the requested GPU(s) don't free up (0 or omitted: wait
forever). This only bounds the wait, not how long
.I command
itself is allowed to run once GPU(s) are acquired.
.RE
.TP
.B status
Show the state of every GPU: free; busy (with owning user, PID, time
acquired, and command); stale (a lock left behind by a dead process,
which this also clears as a side effect); or untracked (the driver
shows an active process on a GPU
.B excubitor
considers free \(em i.e. someone bypassed it entirely). This is
visibility only; nothing here stops or evicts an untracked process.
.TP
.B clean
Clear your own stale (dead\-process) lock/meta files, e.g. left behind
by a crashed job, and report how many were found.
.B status
already does this same cleanup silently as a side effect;
.B clean
is for when you want to trigger it explicitly and see confirmation.
Only affects entries you own \(em
.I LOCK_DIR
is sticky, so another user's leftovers need
.BR "domestikos gc" .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage. Also available per\-command, e.g.
.BR "excubitor run \-h" .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the installed version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B excubitor run
sets
.B CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES
to the acquired GPU index(es), comma\-separated, for the duration of
.IR command .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success. Non\-zero if the requested GPU(s) couldn't be acquired
(e.g. a
.B \-t
timeout), arguments are invalid, or
.I command
itself exits non\-zero.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lock/excubitor/gpu<N>.lock
Per\-GPU lock file backing
.BR flock (1).
.TP
.I /var/lock/excubitor/gpu<N>.meta
Per\-GPU metadata (owning user, PID, start time, command) shown by
.B excubitor status
while the corresponding lock is held.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
excubitor run \-n 1 \-\- python train.py
excubitor run \-n 2 \-t 600 \-\- python train_multi.py
excubitor run \-g 0,2 \-\- python train.py
excubitor status
excubitor clean
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR domestikos (1),
.BR flock (1),
.BR nvidia\-smi (1)
.SH BUGS
Report problems to the administrators via the "FCC SW Machines"
Mattermost channel: https://mattermost.web.cern.ch/fccsw/channels/fccsw-machines
